00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.421
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Where was that switch?

00:05:41.421 --> 00:05:46.093
&gt;&gt; So, I started full quarter at Stanford
ready to start taking all the classes

00:05:46.093 --> 00:05:50.985
about entrepreneur, ready to enjoy my
two years of partying and learning, and

00:05:50.985 --> 00:05:52.270
thinking.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.313
And then one afternoon,
David George was around,

00:05:55.313 --> 00:05:59.699
tells me you gotta go meet Doug Leone
at Sequoia right here at Vista Doug and

00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:03.340
he's they're thinking about
investing in Latin America.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.380
And I, of course, went and
met Doug at Sequoia.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:11.726
And a lot of things were very
odd about the first encounter,

00:06:11.726 --> 00:06:16.726
generally because your first interview
in any place generally is with

00:06:16.726 --> 00:06:20.709
the most junior associate,
with the youngest person,

00:06:20.709 --> 00:06:26.900
here I was talking directly with the head
of the firm and that was not usual.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:31.044
And then I sit in front of the Doug and
we had just a great conversation for

00:06:31.044 --> 00:06:36.020
60 minutes, lot of conversations about
family, a lot of clarity about personal,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:39.361
very uncharacteristics of
venture capital interview.

00:06:39.361 --> 00:06:44.258
I'd later realized that it was all
a psychological profiling that was

00:06:44.258 --> 00:06:47.578
happening at that point [LAUGH] and
Doug told me,

00:06:47.578 --> 00:06:52.147
come back meet our people at Sequoia and
let's see what happens.

00:06:52.147 --> 00:06:56.009
And between between me leaving Sequoia and
getting into my car,

00:06:56.009 --> 00:07:01.040
I already had an email from Michael Moritz
saying, come back I want to meet you.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:05.736
It took five minutes for Doug to talk to
Mike and Mike had sent me back, and so

00:07:05.736 --> 00:07:08.347
in two weeks, I went and met everybody and

00:07:08.347 --> 00:07:13.150
they proposed me to help Sequoia
look at Latin America and Brazil.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:16.473
And so, at that point I wanted to
I wanted to start a business but

00:07:16.473 --> 00:07:20.421
this was just too good of an opportunity
to say no to, I was going to be able to be

00:07:20.421 --> 00:07:23.750
closer to an entrepreneurship and
venture capital investor,

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:27.387
I was going to be able to be very close
to some of the best entrepreneurs in

00:07:27.387 --> 00:07:32.022
the world that operated around this area,
seeing that, being there as they pitched.

00:07:32.022 --> 00:07:36.737
I saw Sequoia, saw the opportunity
was too good to pass, and

00:07:36.737 --> 00:07:41.085
decided to put once again,
pause to the startup idea and

00:07:41.085 --> 00:07:45.960
spend effectively those two
years working at Sequoia.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:46.681
&gt;&gt; How did you balance that?

00:07:46.681 --> 00:07:50.535
For the current students here,
what was the day like?

00:07:50.535 --> 00:07:53.989
&gt;&gt; It was incredibly intense.

00:07:53.989 --> 00:07:59.370
There was an advantage that
Brazil is five hours ahead.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.612
So, I had to wake up at
4:00 in the morning, so

00:08:02.612 --> 00:08:05.560
that Brazil was at 9:00 and 10:00.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:10.394
I was in the Sequoia office at 4:00, 4:30,
calling entrepreneurs and talking and

00:08:10.394 --> 00:08:11.598
sourcing like crazy.

00:08:11.598 --> 00:08:15.511
And so, I was working at
Sequoia from 5:00 to 8 AM,

00:08:15.511 --> 00:08:19.176
would come to GSB,
would have all the classes done,

00:08:19.176 --> 00:08:24.160
then go back to Sequoia until,
I don't know, 6:00, 7 PM.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.810
Then go back home and
try to do some homework.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:34.065
I think the video said that somehow I was
perhaps close to RJ Miller's color, no.

00:08:34.065 --> 00:08:35.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:36.660
&gt;&gt; Not even close.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.360
You had to sacrifice something.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:44.230
And I quickly sacrifice,
this does not make any sense.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:45.790
I cannot do that.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:48.960
I'm not going to do 100% of the readings,
impossible.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.878
I'll pick and I'll take the risk of
the professor calling me on a class and

00:08:53.878 --> 00:08:58.220
not having the answer,
because I didn't read everything.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:08:59.282
I'll embarrassed myself.

00:08:59.282 --> 00:09:02.055
But you have to give something up,
you cannot do it all.

00:09:02.055 --> 00:09:06.360
And that's what I ended up giving up.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:12.470
And there were no Tuesday night party for
me.

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:14.203
Wednesdays were the day off.

00:09:14.203 --> 00:09:18.972
And they were some of the most kind of
really surreal experiences of getting

00:09:18.972 --> 00:09:21.544
picked up at GSB, going to the airport,

00:09:21.544 --> 00:09:24.436
get into a plane with Doug,
fly to Sao Paulo.

00:09:24.436 --> 00:09:29.089
Landing Wednesday 8:00 AM, having ten
meetings, signing four term sheets,

00:09:29.089 --> 00:09:32.230
get back to Sao Paulo, and
be back on Thursday for GSB.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:37.154
So, I would pinch myself being in a plane
saying, what it's happening here?

00:09:37.154 --> 00:09:39.869
But anyway, it was super intense, but

00:09:39.869 --> 00:09:44.489
you had to be able to balance it somehow,
to make everything work.

00:09:44.489 --> 00:09:48.633
&gt;&gt; Right, you talk about giving something
up, you end up giving up investing,

00:09:48.633 --> 00:09:50.311
right and making that switch.

00:09:50.311 --> 00:09:51.530
What was that decision?

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:54.343
What was going through your mind?

00:09:54.343 --> 00:09:59.115
&gt;&gt; So there was a really good advice I
got from Doug at some point that made

00:09:59.115 --> 00:10:00.596
me thought a lot, and

00:10:00.596 --> 00:10:06.216
kind of conceptualize something that I had
already been thinking for a long time.

00:10:06.216 --> 00:10:08.020
And I'll just give
an example talk about that.

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.345
I remember right before business school
going to a conference in New York,

00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:16.891
a venture capital conference,
Latin America venture capital conferences.

00:10:16.891 --> 00:10:19.708
And seeing one day eight
private equity funds in

00:10:19.708 --> 00:10:21.900
Latin America pitching their fund.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:27.193
At the end of the day, it was like
every single pitch is exactly the same.

00:10:27.193 --> 00:10:31.185
There was no differentiation,
everybody was pitching the same strategy.

00:10:31.185 --> 00:10:34.753
And then there was an advice at some
point that I heard from Doug is,

00:10:34.753 --> 00:10:39.235
and it was very also timely about what's
going on in Silicon Valley in 2012 is.

00:10:39.235 --> 00:10:43.844
That you want to position yourself in
the side of the scarcity of the market,

00:10:43.844 --> 00:10:45.980
not in the side of the oversupply.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:49.040
And where was a lot of oversupply?

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:52.102
There was a lot of oversupply of
investors at that point in Latin America

00:10:52.102 --> 00:10:53.510
trying to do.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.940
There was a lot of oversupply
of people like me in the US.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:01.534
And so, me staying in the US was
being positioned in the area where I

00:11:01.534 --> 00:11:05.298
was a commodity,
I was a complete commodity in the US.

00:11:05.298 --> 00:11:09.320
My background what I knew was too many
competent people in Latin America.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:12.770
That's not true,
there is a scarcity of talent.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:16.840
And then where's even more scarcity,
we went and made a lot of startups.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:21.828
There were few startups and the few
startups that they were trying to do were

00:11:21.828 --> 00:11:24.290
almost like clones of US companies.

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:26.808
They were not really solving
the real Latin American problems.

00:11:26.808 --> 00:11:34.520
When we went there were 3,000
clones of Groupon in that time.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:38.297
That's not the biggest problem of
Latin American, is figuring that out.

00:11:38.297 --> 00:11:42.348
That's not what people really care about
when you don't have banking access,

00:11:42.348 --> 00:11:45.673
when there are no hospitals,
when their education is horrible,

00:11:45.673 --> 00:11:47.500
when there is bad infrastructure.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.131
So there were all these
gigantic problems and

00:11:50.131 --> 00:11:54.364
there were no intrapreneurs actually
solving them or addressing them.

00:11:54.364 --> 00:11:56.864
So when you combine all of that it's,

00:11:56.864 --> 00:12:01.868
this is where their scarcity lies is
these huge challenges in Latin America I

00:12:01.868 --> 00:12:06.338
can position my service strategically
to be able to address those.

00:12:06.338 --> 00:12:10.509
And ultimately that meant also stopping
investing and ultimately going

00:12:10.509 --> 00:12:14.968
as an entrepreneur to build something
that was very different at that stage.

00:12:14.968 --> 00:12:18.858
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so you mentioned you
wanted to solve difficult problems,

00:12:18.858 --> 00:12:21.710
you chose banking which
is highly regulated.

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:22.383
In particular,

00:12:22.383 --> 00:12:25.530
Latin America has a lot of entrenched
players in particular in Brazil.

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:26.173
Why banking and

00:12:26.173 --> 00:12:29.950
what were the VCs telling you about
that when you were pitching that idea?

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:35.376
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, couple of reflections
I think I had some time.

00:12:35.376 --> 00:12:39.888
Going back to what really were driving me.

00:12:39.888 --> 00:12:43.154
The big question was, what it impact?

00:12:43.154 --> 00:12:47.572
How can I optimize the amount
of impact per unit of time?

00:12:47.572 --> 00:12:51.270
I'm going to start a business,
its going to take decades, right.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:55.310
It's going to take as much energy to
do something small than something big.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:13:00.179
And when I asked the question, what is
the single hardest thing I can possibly

00:13:00.179 --> 00:13:03.790
imagine, what is the single most
impactful thing I can possibly imagine?

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:06.858
Banks was that answer.

00:13:06.858 --> 00:13:11.324
Because when you look at Latin America
the biggest companies in Brazil are banks,

00:13:11.324 --> 00:13:13.719
the biggest companies in Mexico are banks,

00:13:13.719 --> 00:13:16.327
the biggest companies
in Argentina are banks.

00:13:16.327 --> 00:13:20.371
There's nothing bigger,
it was the hardest thing I could imagine.

00:13:20.371 --> 00:13:24.913
It was the single most impactful thing I
could imagine because it's an oligopoly

00:13:24.913 --> 00:13:30.420
structure is, you have five banks that
dominate 80, 90% of all these countries.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:32.860
When you have an oligopoly,
you have no competition.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:36.754
That translates into some of the highest
interest rates and fees in the world,

00:13:36.754 --> 00:13:38.840
really horrible experience.

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.946
And significant percent of the population
not having access to anybody,

00:13:41.946 --> 00:13:44.911
not being able to get into bank account,
not being able to serve them.

00:13:44.911 --> 00:13:49.686
So if I managed to figure out how to
bring more competition to that industry

00:13:49.686 --> 00:13:53.844
make it 5, 10% better by
competing with these five giants,

00:13:53.844 --> 00:13:58.618
then the impact of that was going to
spread around to entire region, right,

00:13:58.618 --> 00:14:01.103
going to be really, really impactful.

00:14:01.103 --> 00:14:07.071
So I arrived to that from a very high end
thinking about that impacting question,

00:14:07.071 --> 00:14:09.250
no idea really how to do it.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:12.319
It seemed impossible.

00:14:12.319 --> 00:14:17.425
I think I had 30 meetings, coffees in Sao
Paulo with the former CEO of this bank,

00:14:17.425 --> 00:14:19.209
the former CEO of that bank.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:24.889
The overwhelming consensus is, no,
you cannot compete with these banks,

00:14:24.889 --> 00:14:26.780
it's impossible.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:30.480
These are the banks that are the most
powerful corporations in Latin America.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.480
They are controlled by the wealthiest
families in Latin America.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.350
They will never let you compete.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:36.740
They will go after your kids.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.860
They will go after your family.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:41.321
Lot of people had done it, but

00:14:41.321 --> 00:14:46.147
then a lot of arguments that
just didn't make any sense and

00:14:46.147 --> 00:14:51.192
I would listen very carefully
trying to filter out the noes.

00:14:51.192 --> 00:14:55.353
Because there was real
reality behind the noes and

00:14:55.353 --> 00:15:01.200
the noes that were just ultimately
a symptom of consensus and fear.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:05.696
And there were things like, well,
iTau tried to do an online bank in 99 and

00:15:05.696 --> 00:15:08.813
they failed,
that's where you're going to fail.

00:15:08.813 --> 00:15:13.466
And I was, we're in 2012,
60 million people have a smartphone,

00:15:13.466 --> 00:15:18.273
99, you have 5% Internet penetration,
and that was the argument that

00:15:18.273 --> 00:15:22.186
the experts were using to dominate
that people were not use.

00:15:22.186 --> 00:15:26.319
Our internet in Brazil is very slow and
then you had 100 million people already

00:15:26.319 --> 00:15:30.410
use being Brazil, top five in the world
in Instagram and Facebook and YouTube.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:36.557
So a lot of arguments that the experts
were proposing against that idea.

00:15:36.557 --> 00:15:39.358
But when you think a little
bit deeply about them,

00:15:39.358 --> 00:15:41.434
they would be very weak underneath.

00:15:41.434 --> 00:15:46.066
And ultimately, my conclusion at the end
of maybe two months of talking to people

00:15:46.066 --> 00:15:48.118
was, wow, there's so much fear.

00:15:48.118 --> 00:15:53.382
There's, so much fear about competing
with that, that's all what is driving.

00:15:53.382 --> 00:15:56.769
And obviously technology was
switching the opportunity,

00:15:56.769 --> 00:15:59.540
if we had been 20 Tend to
compete with those banks.

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:03.544
I needed a billion dollars to
put branches in every corner and

00:16:03.544 --> 00:16:08.470
buy IBM mainframes the right time
called the why now question.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:10.788
Smartphone removed all those barriers and

00:16:10.788 --> 00:16:14.210
allowed us to use technology
to reach 100% of population.

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:18.596
So it was a confluence of technology
at getting to the right time, but

00:16:18.596 --> 00:16:22.752
then also kind of as an outsider,
and I saw myself as an outsider,

00:16:22.752 --> 00:16:27.690
I'm not Brazilian, I never worked for
a bank in Brazil, I was not consumed.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:31.537
They did not grew up in that environment
where people were fearful of these big

00:16:31.537 --> 00:16:35.267
companies, I didn't even know a lot
of these controlling families that my

00:16:35.267 --> 00:16:37.890
Brazilian friends knew and feared.

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:42.410
Having been an outsider looking in ended
up being a huge advantage because allowed

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:44.767
me to disentangle a lot
of the arguments and

00:16:44.767 --> 00:16:48.345
ultimately realized that there
could be an opportunity there.

00:16:48.345 --> 00:16:49.028
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; But

00:16:49.028 --> 00:16:53.235
from that idea to traction is a long road,
right?

00:16:53.235 --> 00:16:55.585
When did you know that you are getting
traction with the consumers?

00:16:55.585 --> 00:16:57.115
The ones making the decisions every day?

00:16:57.115 --> 00:17:00.335
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so at every stage.

00:17:00.335 --> 00:17:03.315
Yeah, we got the first
million from Sequoia.

00:17:03.315 --> 00:17:10.340
It was a huge bet in the region
on the Reggie bar we dug.

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:13.121
That helped with Casa
could put the R million,

00:17:13.121 --> 00:17:17.398
but there were so many open questions
that I couldn't even answer, and

00:17:17.398 --> 00:17:20.840
there were so
many points of perhaps complete failures.

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:24.870
So for example,
there were two issues of credit cards.

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:28.815
One of them, when we went to them, and
said, hey, we're starting this FinTech Neo

00:17:28.815 --> 00:17:31.830
bank said, you crazy, no way,
we're not going to work with you.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:36.597
One left and
when we went to them they said yes,

00:17:36.597 --> 00:17:40.560
so if they had said no, we're done.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:45.033
There were changes in regulation that came
in and at every single step there were

00:17:45.033 --> 00:17:48.720
points of failure but somehow with
a small team we were creative.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:54.335
I remember there was a new law that
appeared in Brazil, a new regulation

00:17:54.335 --> 00:18:00.430
that effectively forced us to launch
three months before in April 2014.

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:05.411
We were going to launch first card in June
and the regulation change that said if

00:18:05.411 --> 00:18:10.167
you're not operating April 2013 you
will need a banking license which

00:18:10.167 --> 00:18:14.868
means it will take you three years to
get it, which means you'll be dead.

00:18:14.868 --> 00:18:19.699
So we grabbed the entire team and
said forget the timeline, survival mode,

00:18:19.699 --> 00:18:22.652
April we have to be live and
we have a timeline.

00:18:22.652 --> 00:18:26.908
And it was so crazy that to be able
to match the timeline perfectly,

00:18:26.908 --> 00:18:30.784
we had to get an approval from
MasterCard the right time, and

00:18:30.784 --> 00:18:33.220
there was only approving in Holland.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:38.849
FedEx was going to take two more days than
me flying in an airplane to Holland and

00:18:38.849 --> 00:18:42.750
giving that sheet in the headquarters
of MasterCard there.

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:45.940
So we would get in a plane in flight
to Holland to give one pitch to

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:49.920
somebody MasterCard, because that
will save us today from FedEx.

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:53.458
So that was the level of urgency of
trying to figure it out how we survive so

00:18:53.458 --> 00:18:56.160
we could launch those products at time.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:57.080
So we launched in time.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:03.781
We had the 12 cards up and running,
we had 12 employees, went to the corner

00:19:03.781 --> 00:19:08.999
shop paid some coffee obviously as
always didn't work the first product.

00:19:08.999 --> 00:19:14.104
Everybody's disappointed, everybody
be sad when at home what's happening,

00:19:14.104 --> 00:19:17.290
eventually we iterated worked out.

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:21.892
And then we raised the serious A and then
that was going to be the coming out kind

00:19:21.892 --> 00:19:25.330
of announcement we're
going to have do a lot of PR.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:28.810
And we're going to open the company for
external consumers.

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:34.339
And the day we announced, as we were
saying, and we opened the website,

00:19:34.339 --> 00:19:40.310
we had an internal bet about how
many customers are going to sign up.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:45.200
And somebody said 1,500, 1,000,
somebody else said 10,000.

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:48.094
The average of the employees was 1,000.

00:19:48.094 --> 00:19:51.907
We announced we worked really
hard to get ourselves or

00:19:51.907 --> 00:19:55.110
named there in the big magazine.

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:55.865
We got 200.

00:19:55.865 --> 00:19:59.347
Completely surprised
everybody went home sigh,

00:19:59.347 --> 00:20:05.310
my god what's going to happen, people
don't want that is everything is wrong.

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:11.805
And then a couple three months happen
we were getting 100, 50 people there.

00:20:11.805 --> 00:20:16.092
As the year passed, and
then there was one one day,

00:20:16.092 --> 00:20:19.700
unexpected one very niche publication.

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:24.121
That was not the main newspaper,
one niche publication that went after

00:20:24.121 --> 00:20:28.550
the design community and
engineer community, talked about the card.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:32.473
And suddenly the following
day we got 3,000 people, and

00:20:32.473 --> 00:20:36.057
the following day we got 6,000,
and the following day is 10,000.

00:20:36.057 --> 00:20:40.266
And suddenly growth just started going
out of control to the point where we were

00:20:40.266 --> 00:20:43.967
completely unprepared and
we created this concept of waiting list,

00:20:43.967 --> 00:20:48.304
which then created even more scarcity,
which meant more people wanted the car,

00:20:48.304 --> 00:20:50.120
which created more.

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.467
So that's when we started to think
that when that publication came in and

00:20:54.467 --> 00:20:57.470
all these people started I was,
whoa, okay?

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:58.850
There's something here.

00:20:58.850 --> 00:20:59.650
Yeah.

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:03.009
&gt;&gt; Well, so it's incredible to hear this
journey and there's a lot of budding

00:21:03.009 --> 00:21:06.683
entrepreneurs in the audience like let's
check who here is working on a business or

00:21:06.683 --> 00:21:10.180
thinks they're going to work in
a business after graduation.

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.620
Forget us even more than I thought.

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:18.572
What's one thing you know now that
you wish you knew when you were

00:21:18.572 --> 00:21:19.583
sitting out there?

00:21:22.473 --> 00:21:25.440
&gt;&gt; So, a lot of different things.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:29.850
I think we did when we
looked at that industry and

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:34.922
when all these experts told us no or
approach was forget

00:21:34.922 --> 00:21:39.680
experience, we don't
want experienced people.

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:40.790
We want complete outsiders.

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:43.900
We want to reinvent everything for
first principles.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.162
Everything, from the way
the product works, collections,

00:21:47.162 --> 00:21:51.190
we did not want hire somebody from
the collection agencies of the big banks.

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:53.360
We reinvented collection from scratch.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:56.080
We did not want these core banking
systems of the [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:57.800
We built a core banking
system from scratch.

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:02.580
So it was all about reinventing
the entire industry from scratch.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.430
And that meant hiring people
that are completely outsiders.

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:07.830
That worked well.

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:09.162
But that did not scale and

00:22:09.162 --> 00:22:12.600
that became our significant
bottleneck as we started scaling.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:15.885
And that also ultimately meant
reinventing the wheel in a number of

00:22:15.885 --> 00:22:19.240
different things that frankly,
we just didn't need to reinvent.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:25.489
There was a lot of good people in
collection that could bring they won and

00:22:25.489 --> 00:22:31.430
saved us a year of reinventing
collection from scratch.

00:22:31.430 --> 00:22:35.820
So that complete dismissal of
experience was too extreme.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:39.870
Ultimately, I think what we learned was
you needed to figure out how to find

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:44.020
the right balance between
the outsiders and the insiders.

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:50.470
How to find the insiders that we're
still able to think as a beginner.

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:53.480
We always say that we like to hire people
that have more questions than answers.

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:56.459
And we continue to look for
those people that have more questions and

00:22:56.459 --> 00:22:58.236
answers than the person that comes in and

00:22:58.236 --> 00:23:00.550
has all answers that has
three years of experience.

00:23:00.550 --> 00:23:02.610
We don't want that person even know.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:06.006
But even the insiders that
can ask little questions,

00:23:06.006 --> 00:23:09.867
they're very valuable because
they know the enemy within.

00:23:09.867 --> 00:23:12.198
They saw all the vulnerabilities,

00:23:12.198 --> 00:23:17.091
they saw the things that could be better,
but they can still have the kind of

00:23:17.091 --> 00:23:21.610
the beginner's mind of reinventing
a lot of products from scratch.

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:25.883
And so where we've increasingly dawn and
as we scaled organization we've been

00:23:25.883 --> 00:23:29.596
actively more looking for
those insiders that can help us reinvent.

00:23:29.596 --> 00:23:32.907
And especially as you start
really tackling scale problems,

00:23:32.907 --> 00:23:35.453
how do you build engineering
systems at scale,

00:23:35.453 --> 00:23:38.700
how do you build customer service
at scale the data science,

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:43.223
machine learning systems at scale, then
you need to rely much more inexperienced,

00:23:43.223 --> 00:23:46.700
doesn't make a lot of sense
to be trying to reinvent.

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:48.230
So it's all a balance.

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:52.935
But I would say having been able to find
the right balance earlier would have saved

00:23:52.935 --> 00:23:54.400
us a lot of pain, right?

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:57.296
So you mentioned people and I want to
talk about something related to that,

00:23:57.296 --> 00:23:59.270
which I know you talk a lot
about which is culture.

00:24:00.764 --> 00:24:04.146
You talk about having kind of
the founding team and expanding.

00:24:04.146 --> 00:24:12.267
How do you maintain the NewBank culture
that the company is so famous for?

00:24:12.267 --> 00:24:16.015
&gt;&gt; So I mentioned earlier how impressed
at Buzz, I was with Sequoia and

00:24:16.015 --> 00:24:18.927
how a lot of the things at Sequoia were so
different.

00:24:18.927 --> 00:24:24.546
And one of the things that I reflected
while I was doing the internship is,

00:24:24.546 --> 00:24:29.818
I had never felt myself so much weight
in my entire life with any work.

00:24:29.818 --> 00:24:36.095
And in previous jobs, I felt that I was
always at maybe 80% of my capacity,

00:24:36.095 --> 00:24:41.520
90% my capacity at the beginning
of being pushed and learning.

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:46.393
At Sequoia in the first few months,
I was 100, it was clear to me

00:24:46.393 --> 00:24:51.630
there was nothing more, and so
it made me think, what is driving this?

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:56.239
What's different about this place that is
driving somebody like me to want to go at

00:24:56.239 --> 00:25:00.329
this pace and working for Doug,
what's driving me to go at this speed?

00:25:00.329 --> 00:25:05.660
And [LAUGH] exactly, it was the resolute.

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:10.178
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it wasn't that, that's amazing,

00:25:10.178 --> 00:25:11.871
it's not the whip.

00:25:11.871 --> 00:25:14.987
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; First,

00:25:14.987 --> 00:25:19.834
it was this sense of ownership, I was an
intern from business school, and the head

00:25:19.834 --> 00:25:24.206
of the firm in investment committee
asking me what I thought about a deal.

00:25:24.206 --> 00:25:28.193
Nobody in my previous experience
that had asked me what I thought,

00:25:28.193 --> 00:25:29.555
I was just an analyst.

00:25:29.555 --> 00:25:33.082
In the first investment committee at
Sequoia as an intern, I sat next to Doug

00:25:33.082 --> 00:25:36.240
and he asked me what I thought, and
I didn't have an opinion ready.

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:39.992
And that meant that for
the next one, I better be prepared,

00:25:39.992 --> 00:25:42.481
I better knew how to give a good answer.

00:25:42.481 --> 00:25:46.815
And so that level of autonomy with
ownership was a really strong

00:25:46.815 --> 00:25:50.348
commendation that how flat
the organization was.

00:25:50.348 --> 00:25:53.516
It was not all these layers
where the CEO was over there,

00:25:53.516 --> 00:25:55.820
it was like everybody had the same room.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:58.672
Remember Mike Moritz,
he didn't even have an office,

00:25:58.672 --> 00:26:01.592
he got us a chair in the hall and
you're talking with him.

00:26:01.592 --> 00:26:06.101
So that to me, that was culture and
culture was driving that motivation.

00:26:06.101 --> 00:26:11.003
And the other thing I heard a lot
about Sequoia that I was able to learn

00:26:11.003 --> 00:26:15.648
when I was sitting in front of this
entrepreneur was hearing a lot

00:26:15.648 --> 00:26:18.400
of saying that the culture of a company

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:23.156
was built in the first six months
by the first 10 to 15 people.

00:26:23.156 --> 00:26:28.181
So when I started NewBank, the first
thing I did was the pitch deck for

00:26:28.181 --> 00:26:32.266
the sale but the second thing
I did was the culture deck.

00:26:32.266 --> 00:26:35.029
And this culture deck was
like the constitution,

00:26:35.029 --> 00:26:38.530
what are the values of this company
we're about to begin to do?

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:41.780
And had a lot of elements that
I had learned at Sequoia,

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:45.809
the level of ownership, this autonomy,
this flat organization,

00:26:45.809 --> 00:26:49.500
the view that I personally thought
drove a lot of motivation.

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:54.541
And since the first 10, 15 employees were
so important, as we hired them with my

00:26:54.541 --> 00:26:59.511
co-founder, we were very actively looking
for people that had brought that DNA,

00:26:59.511 --> 00:27:03.287
and we co-designed this culture
deck with everybody of them.

00:27:03.287 --> 00:27:05.565
From the designer to the first engineer,

00:27:05.565 --> 00:27:08.637
they all participated in
the construction of the deck.

00:27:08.637 --> 00:27:11.873
And this was very powerful
because in a way,

00:27:11.873 --> 00:27:16.961
sometimes I think about it as
the Constitution of the United States.

00:27:16.961 --> 00:27:21.853
The Constitution of the United States
is over 200 years, it's very short,

00:27:21.853 --> 00:27:26.161
it represents what the US as a country
stands for, and here we are, and

00:27:26.161 --> 00:27:28.077
people still go drawn to that.

00:27:28.077 --> 00:27:30.400
And for us that deck was
the constitution of NewBank.

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:34.784
And today those values are as
latent as important for

00:27:34.784 --> 00:27:38.381
us as they were when
we were ten employees.

00:27:38.381 --> 00:27:43.089
So that has scaled a lot, that gave us a
lot of clarity around the people we wanted

00:27:43.089 --> 00:27:47.892
to hire, about the way we did performance
management, the way we let go people.

00:27:47.892 --> 00:27:51.077
That drove all decisions,
that drove product decisions,

00:27:51.077 --> 00:27:55.099
that drove the type of language we should
use in an email with the consumer.

00:27:55.099 --> 00:27:59.929
Our focus number one is this obsession
about consumers that meant hours of debate

00:27:59.929 --> 00:28:05.058
about the words that we could not use with
a consumer, because they were too complex.

00:28:05.058 --> 00:28:09.886
About the level of simplicity, this
ownership of everybody flagged that meant

00:28:09.886 --> 00:28:14.714
customers' calls would go initially to
my co-founder Christina's phone and

00:28:14.714 --> 00:28:16.818
my phone, I would get customers.

00:28:16.818 --> 00:28:18.504
The first caller was not
a customer service person,

00:28:18.504 --> 00:28:19.507
I was getting them in my phone.

00:28:19.507 --> 00:28:23.041
So I think ultimately that
kind of became a bit of

00:28:23.041 --> 00:28:27.132
the realization where
culture is the driving force.

00:28:27.132 --> 00:28:31.487
There's nothing more important because
culture allows you to hire people,

00:28:31.487 --> 00:28:34.709
people build products,
products bring you customers.

00:28:34.709 --> 00:28:39.637
And so ultimately, consumers don't
come to you because of your products,

00:28:39.637 --> 00:28:42.642
consumers come to you
because of the culture.

00:28:42.642 --> 00:28:47.303
They are consumers of culture,
they're not consumers of products.

00:28:47.303 --> 00:28:51.595
And really authentic companies
are companies that are able to really

00:28:51.595 --> 00:28:54.860
ultimately represent and
show what they stand for.

00:28:54.860 --> 00:28:58.616
And obviously, more importantly,
they have integrity,

00:28:58.616 --> 00:29:02.460
they do what they say they do,
there's perfect alignment.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:07.573
And this is perhaps as you scale the
product would be much more mindful about,

00:29:07.573 --> 00:29:11.837
the expanding culture has meant
we have a number of the routine.

00:29:11.837 --> 00:29:15.129
For example,
this culture deck that I mentioned,

00:29:15.129 --> 00:29:20.118
I still present the culture every month
to everybody that starts at NewBank.

00:29:20.118 --> 00:29:24.641
Since the beginning till now, it's me,
I don't delegate that to anybody.

00:29:24.641 --> 00:29:28.326
Every month I present this culture deck
with the values to every single person,

00:29:28.326 --> 00:29:31.419
from the customer service,
the receptionist, to the engineer.

00:29:31.419 --> 00:29:33.579
They all go through this
onboarding session with me,

00:29:33.579 --> 00:29:34.830
where I present them the deck.

00:29:34.830 --> 00:29:40.091
And I think that shows the level set,
the lack of hierarchy, the transparency.

00:29:40.091 --> 00:29:43.665
That shows how important culture is,
the values.

00:29:43.665 --> 00:29:48.291
But then every single time thinking
about the decisions you make,

00:29:48.291 --> 00:29:51.024
you have to bring back those values in.

00:29:51.024 --> 00:29:57.530
So I'll give one last example
just to exemplify this.

00:29:57.530 --> 00:30:00.981
About a year ago,
one team came back and said, wow,

00:30:00.981 --> 00:30:05.380
something is happening,
we're making more money per customer.

00:30:05.380 --> 00:30:09.208
Suddenly the cohorts,
the economics start increasing,

00:30:09.208 --> 00:30:11.872
you see a bleep of revenue per customer.

00:30:11.872 --> 00:30:14.930
We went and looked and
tried to figure out what had happened.

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:17.793
And it turns out that
through a bug in a system,

00:30:17.793 --> 00:30:22.428
an engineer had removed an email that
reminded the customer to pay on time.

00:30:22.428 --> 00:30:27.800
And obviously customers were being late
more and we were charging more late fees.

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:31.615
It was very simple for
everybody to know what to do,

00:30:31.615 --> 00:30:37.760
because we had a framework of values that
provided context around that decision.

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:41.318
The right thing to do was,
not only go back and put that email, but

00:30:41.318 --> 00:30:45.009
also go back to customers that have
overpaid and tell them, sorry,

00:30:45.009 --> 00:30:48.993
we made a mistake, we removed this
email that you should have received.

00:30:48.993 --> 00:30:53.353
We're not going to charge you this fee,
and here's your money back.

00:30:53.353 --> 00:30:56.917
And that to me was a really
powerful example because first,

00:30:56.917 --> 00:31:00.351
99% of companies would have
done the exact opposite.

00:31:00.351 --> 00:31:04.153
They actually would have said, let's
see what our email we'll remove, right?

00:31:04.153 --> 00:31:05.684
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is great,

00:31:05.684 --> 00:31:09.638
let's take this email out, let's take
this email out to make more money.

00:31:09.638 --> 00:31:13.962
Even companies that say they're consumer
obsessed would probably have had

00:31:13.962 --> 00:31:17.474
a lot of really tough conversation
of somebody saying [SOUND],

00:31:17.474 --> 00:31:21.001
the customer should have known,
it was his responsibility.

00:31:21.001 --> 00:31:25.209
For us it was both clarity,
we want customer obsession,

00:31:25.209 --> 00:31:28.374
put that money back, put that email back.

00:31:28.374 --> 00:31:31.955
And then the answer, ultimately is
a teaching moment for everybody,

00:31:31.955 --> 00:31:35.794
is like, why does it make sense and
why is it consistent with the business?

00:31:35.794 --> 00:31:40.274
Because when you're a consumer and
your bank is telling you this, you say,

00:31:40.274 --> 00:31:44.404
I will never go anywhere else for
the next decade, you got me forever,

00:31:44.404 --> 00:31:46.309
nobody else will do that for me.

00:31:46.309 --> 00:31:49.529
And that exemplifies very well I think,
what we think,

00:31:49.529 --> 00:31:53.720
which is ultimately this culture is
aligned with business objectives.

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:56.331
We get to retain that customer for
a decade now.

00:31:56.331 --> 00:32:00.421
Because we reimbursed
a dollar in a late fee.

00:32:00.421 --> 00:32:04.860
So ultimately, that is a very
powerful example internally, and

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:10.281
shows the integrity of the culture,
which is actions speak louder than words.

00:32:10.281 --> 00:32:13.795
And this example ultimately
exemplify that very well.

00:32:13.795 --> 00:32:16.337
&gt;&gt; Yeah, want to switch gears a second and

00:32:16.337 --> 00:32:21.189
talk about Latin America, a region
that's close to both of our hearts.

00:32:21.189 --> 00:32:24.593
As you mentioned, Sequoia pulled the plug
in Latin America in 2012 because of

00:32:24.593 --> 00:32:26.530
the lack of tech and
entrepreneurial talent.

00:32:26.530 --> 00:32:29.249
And last year,
they came back into Latin America.

00:32:29.249 --> 00:32:34.572
2017, Nubank opened an office in
Berlin to attract tech talent, right?

00:32:34.572 --> 00:32:39.561
And now we have a classmate of mine out in
this audience somewhere, who's gringo as

00:32:39.561 --> 00:32:43.859
gringo gets, and he's moving to
Sao Paulo next year to join your team.

00:32:43.859 --> 00:32:50.438
There's a transformation happening,
but how far do we still have to go?

00:32:51.462 --> 00:32:55.927
&gt;&gt; So it's early days,
there is a lot of work ahead.

00:32:55.927 --> 00:33:03.397
I think, the future of every industry in
every country is technology companies.

00:33:03.397 --> 00:33:07.897
There is no way back, and I think it was
obvious in retail, initially with Amazon,

00:33:07.897 --> 00:33:12.683
and then companies like Netflix in media,
and companies like Uber in transportation.

00:33:12.683 --> 00:33:16.733
Every single vertical of
the economy will be owned 10, 20,

00:33:16.733 --> 00:33:20.262
30 years from now by new
digitally native companies.

00:33:20.262 --> 00:33:24.456
That means that every single industry you
see today in any country in Latin America

00:33:24.456 --> 00:33:25.870
is going to be reinvented.

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:29.778
And sometimes it'll be the incumbent
that will be able to reinvent.

00:33:29.778 --> 00:33:32.323
That will probably be
the exception on the rules,

00:33:32.323 --> 00:33:36.273
but there is a huge opportunity to
reinvent all economies in all industries.

00:33:36.273 --> 00:33:39.456
Now, who does that reinvention?

00:33:39.456 --> 00:33:43.756
Entrepreneurs, and
very much engineers at the end.

00:33:43.756 --> 00:33:46.867
We're starting to finally get
entrepreneurs in Latin America.

00:33:46.867 --> 00:33:53.780
Hopefully, all of you, even the gringos,
go Latin America and start businesses.

00:33:53.780 --> 00:33:56.179
Because 12 years ago
that wasn't happening,

00:33:56.179 --> 00:34:00.341
people were going to the consulting firms,
people were going to the big companies.

00:34:00.341 --> 00:34:04.181
And the few entrepreneurs were just,
again, looking at the clones,

00:34:04.181 --> 00:34:05.494
looking at this stuff.

00:34:05.494 --> 00:34:09.464
But there was a lot of opportunity,
reinvent banking, reinvent healthcare,

00:34:09.464 --> 00:34:11.906
reinvent telecom, reinvent transportation.

00:34:11.906 --> 00:34:16.470
Now engineers, that's a big, big,
big problem, very unresolved,

00:34:16.470 --> 00:34:18.151
it's one of the reasons.

00:34:18.151 --> 00:34:22.149
If all the industries are going to
be reinvented in Latin America,

00:34:22.149 --> 00:34:26.231
there are two options by tech companies,
there are two options.

00:34:26.231 --> 00:34:30.264
Or they get reinvented by
Latin American technology companies, or

00:34:30.264 --> 00:34:35.020
will be American and Chinese companies
that will be global and will be in LatAm.

00:34:35.020 --> 00:34:37.704
If it's Latin American companies,
you need engineers, and

00:34:37.704 --> 00:34:40.246
there are not engineers in Latin America,
there are not.

00:34:40.246 --> 00:34:43.877
I remember with Doug in
University of Sao Paulo,

00:34:43.877 --> 00:34:48.317
42 computer science engineers
graduating in 2013.

00:34:48.317 --> 00:34:51.844
Brazil graduates,
I think something like 30,000.

00:34:51.844 --> 00:34:54.896
I was looking at the numbers in
Colombia a couple of weeks ago.

00:34:54.896 --> 00:34:57.276
There are 10x more graduates today,

00:34:57.276 --> 00:35:01.972
there are 10x more people studying law
in Colombia than computer science.

00:35:01.972 --> 00:35:03.711
Doesn't make any sense.

00:35:03.711 --> 00:35:08.678
And what's strange is you would think,
initially you would say the lack of this

00:35:08.678 --> 00:35:11.956
bottleneck, you would
think it's a supply issue.

00:35:11.956 --> 00:35:15.511
Nobody's teaching these
people computer engineering.

00:35:15.511 --> 00:35:16.818
It's actually a demand issue.

00:35:16.818 --> 00:35:20.891
These students are graduating
from high school today, and

00:35:20.891 --> 00:35:25.291
they're still choosing to do
business administration or law,

00:35:25.291 --> 00:35:30.178
when there is about half a million
gap of computer scientists in LatAm,

00:35:30.178 --> 00:35:32.800
which is more or less the bottleneck.

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:36.938
So, unclear how to solve that, there is a
big opportunity, there's a big challenge.

00:35:36.938 --> 00:35:39.847
If we don't solve it,
there'll be Chinese and

00:35:39.847 --> 00:35:44.664
US companies owning all industries in
LatAm, because somebody's going to do it.

00:35:44.664 --> 00:35:47.503
If we solve it,
somehow we grab all these people.

00:35:47.503 --> 00:35:52.426
There's a huge opportunity to not only
reinvent all the economies in all

00:35:52.426 --> 00:35:57.188
of these countries, but also to
contribute to solve one of the biggest

00:35:57.188 --> 00:36:01.969
problems that we have, which is
opportunity and income inequality.

00:36:01.969 --> 00:36:05.070
Because if it's just a few
companies from abroad,

00:36:05.070 --> 00:36:10.117
then you have this extreme concentration
of market cap in some of these companies,

00:36:10.117 --> 00:36:13.958
and you don't have the Latin Americans
contributing to that.

00:36:13.958 --> 00:36:17.850
So you will end up with much,
much higher income inequality.

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:21.768
If you solve it, then you have all Latin
Americans building their own programming

00:36:21.768 --> 00:36:22.960
and design and product,

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:26.730
creating the next company that are going
to be reinventing all these regions.

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:31.726
So it's almost like an existential threat,
for all countries right now,

00:36:31.726 --> 00:36:36.500
globally, same thing happens in Africa,
same thing happens in Asia.

00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:40.760
The problem is that, I would say LatAm and
perhaps Africa, we're just farther away.

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:43.309
And even though I think
here at Stanford for us,

00:36:43.309 --> 00:36:47.659
it's almost like it's clear now what's
happening the next 10, 20 years.

00:36:47.659 --> 00:36:52.557
You talk to the students that are
graduating in high school in Guadalajara

00:36:52.557 --> 00:36:56.033
or in Salvador Bahia or
in Cali, they don't see it,

00:36:56.033 --> 00:37:00.150
they don't know what's coming,
they haven't seen it yet.

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:03.875
&gt;&gt; Speaking of inequality,
your wife Maria is here today as well and

00:37:03.875 --> 00:37:06.449
together last year, as mentioned,

00:37:06.449 --> 00:37:10.670
you were only the second Latin American
family to sign the giving pledge.

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:14.020
Take us into the conversation
you were having together.

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:15.027
What motivated that decision?

00:37:15.027 --> 00:37:22.450
&gt;&gt; So, [COUGH] I think, we did Nubank
as I said, because we wanted to build,

00:37:22.450 --> 00:37:28.320
we wanted to problem solve,
we wanted to create impact.

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:34.287
And it was the sheer kind of joy
of creation and creating impact.

00:37:34.287 --> 00:37:36.247
And so money was never a motivation,

00:37:36.247 --> 00:37:40.180
it was never really something that we
thought about, it was not an issue.

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:44.197
Both Maria and I come from middle
class families that gave us a lot of

00:37:44.197 --> 00:37:48.885
opportunity, and we feel very blessed
about the opportunities that we had.

00:37:48.885 --> 00:37:52.795
But money per se was never
really a driving factor.

00:37:52.795 --> 00:37:57.112
And then suddenly these companies
is being worth $1 billion, and 5,

00:37:57.112 --> 00:37:58.894
and 10, and 30 billion.

00:37:58.894 --> 00:38:03.828
And the speed is huge, and
then about 12 months ago

00:38:03.828 --> 00:38:07.989
we raised money at 25 billion valuation.

00:38:07.989 --> 00:38:12.452
And you start making the numbers,
like wow,

00:38:12.452 --> 00:38:14.915
this is a lot of money
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:38:14.915 --> 00:38:17.014
&gt;&gt; And we don't need it,

00:38:17.014 --> 00:38:19.560
we don't need this money.

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:23.360
We don't need it,
we don't live a luxury life.

00:38:23.360 --> 00:38:28.734
We don't need it, and it just feels
like a big responsibility suddenly,

00:38:28.734 --> 00:38:32.820
because going back to impact,
that's what drives us.

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:35.674
And then it becomes a big responsibility.

00:38:35.674 --> 00:38:40.320
How do you use these money to provide
the biggest impact possible individually?

00:38:40.320 --> 00:38:44.641
You go back to the same driving factor,
the impact question.

00:38:44.641 --> 00:38:48.219
And I had read a book that I
recommend everybody to reading,

00:38:48.219 --> 00:38:53.160
it's one of my impactful books called
A Billionaire Who Wasn't, Chuck Feeney,

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:55.754
the founder of Atlantic Philanthropies.

00:38:55.754 --> 00:38:59.723
It's a fascinating book where he goes for
20 years doing philanthropy in the world.

00:38:59.723 --> 00:39:03.340
He decides that he wants to spend it
all while he lives, all the money.

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:07.940
And he says that he wants
his last check to bounce.

00:39:07.940 --> 00:39:12.268
And I just thought that was
the most powerful analogy, and

00:39:12.268 --> 00:39:17.495
when we started thinking about it,
we said, we want to be that person.

00:39:17.495 --> 00:39:21.099
Because if you start thinking about it,
first, what a great opportunity,

00:39:21.099 --> 00:39:22.630
all society is going to change.

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:26.748
And we're been benefiting significantly
from the change of technology.

00:39:26.748 --> 00:39:30.856
But second is it's just going to
drive significant income inequality.

00:39:30.856 --> 00:39:35.704
And so, philanthropy and
social impact, giving back,

00:39:35.704 --> 00:39:38.433
plays a role, but why delay it?

00:39:38.433 --> 00:39:41.961
Why delay this to the end when you're
80 years old, when you're tired,

00:39:41.961 --> 00:39:43.529
when you don't have the energy,

00:39:43.529 --> 00:39:47.003
when you don't necessarily bring
the clarity of mind to solve problems.

00:39:47.003 --> 00:39:48.930
Even worse, why dying and

00:39:48.930 --> 00:39:54.410
leaving all the money to a foundation
of people that will do it?

00:39:54.410 --> 00:39:58.814
Or to your kids where a lot of
the times that is just going to make

00:39:58.814 --> 00:40:01.090
more damage to them than good.

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:04.680
So we decided we do not want to leave
it to the kids, they will be loved.

00:40:04.680 --> 00:40:07.504
They will get a lot of opportunity but-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:07.504 --> 00:40:09.725
&gt;&gt; Leaving a lot of the stuff,

00:40:09.725 --> 00:40:11.855
we don't want to do that.

00:40:11.855 --> 00:40:14.300
So number one, not to the kids.

00:40:14.300 --> 00:40:18.245
They're young and they're not looking
at this but maybe one day they'll be-

00:40:18.245 --> 00:40:19.468
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:40:19.468 --> 00:40:21.269
&gt;&gt; They'll be very resentful, I hope not.

00:40:21.269 --> 00:40:24.180
I hope they'll get to understand
why we're doing this.

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:28.860
So number one, number two,
we're all dying at some point.

00:40:28.860 --> 00:40:32.739
There will be one day where
we won't exist anymore.

00:40:32.739 --> 00:40:38.110
And I think it was Andrew Carnegie who
said it who dies rich dies disgraced,

00:40:38.110 --> 00:40:43.920
because there is so much pain and so much
problems to be solved in the world today.

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:45.500
Why would you delay it?

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:50.480
Why would you not use all that
money now to solve those issues?

00:40:50.480 --> 00:40:55.950
Why would wait decades you if you can do
a make a difference using your energy,

00:40:55.950 --> 00:40:58.657
your mind, and your money to do that?

00:40:58.657 --> 00:41:02.503
Why die and risk not you participating
of the solving of those problems?

00:41:02.503 --> 00:41:07.708
So all of that,
there was a big kind of brainstorming that

00:41:07.708 --> 00:41:12.596
led us to this decision that
we want to give it all away.

00:41:12.596 --> 00:41:19.020
We want our last check to bounce, and
we're going to be thoughtful about it.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:20.390
We're going to set it the right way.

00:41:20.390 --> 00:41:21.750
We're going to build a family foundation.

00:41:21.750 --> 00:41:25.873
We're going to go back and use a lot of
the tools that Stanford and business gives

00:41:25.873 --> 00:41:29.690
us of think back on first principles,
think about all these problems.

00:41:29.690 --> 00:41:31.630
What is driving this inequality?

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:35.833
Where is the highest impacts
per dollar spent in the region?

00:41:35.833 --> 00:41:40.630
And can we over the next four or
five maybe six decades,

00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:46.595
use this money and this energy to
create as much impact as possible?

00:41:46.595 --> 00:41:51.385
And so that's when in the very,
very, very early days of journey,

00:41:51.385 --> 00:41:54.003
it's a very interesting question.

00:41:54.003 --> 00:41:55.870
I think philanthropy
needs to be disrupted.

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:57.699
There is a lot of lessons on,

00:41:57.699 --> 00:42:03.036
we see ourselves with Danielle here who is
also largely as big is helping us, that we

00:42:03.036 --> 00:42:08.327
find ourselves in meetings using concepts
of computer science in philanthropy.

00:42:08.327 --> 00:42:12.399
Talking about platformization, talking
about open source, talking about crypto.

00:42:12.399 --> 00:42:17.168
And there is a lot that can be used
to start addressing some of these big

00:42:17.168 --> 00:42:19.060
problems and ultimately,

00:42:19.060 --> 00:42:23.437
not let this technology become
one more driver of inequality.

00:42:23.437 --> 00:42:29.392
But actually have it be an enabler of more
equality of opportunity for everybody.

00:42:29.392 --> 00:42:34.947
&gt;&gt; Great,
let's actually end on that note for now.

00:42:34.947 --> 00:42:44.947
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:42:46.146 --> 00:42:49.285
&gt;&gt; Incredibly inspiring and
a motive for action.

00:42:49.285 --> 00:42:51.130
I want to go to some audience Q&amp;A.

00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:53.451
I know we have a lot of questions.

00:42:53.451 --> 00:42:57.690
So first questions right
over here from Ben.

00:42:57.690 --> 00:42:58.721
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for being here.

00:42:58.721 --> 00:43:02.859
I'm Ben Goldwater, MBA class of 22.

00:43:02.859 --> 00:43:07.633
One of the reason I'm very excited is that
I'm coaching someone who tells me he wants

00:43:07.633 --> 00:43:09.392
to be the next you in ten years.

00:43:09.392 --> 00:43:14.322
So financial products, there's kind of two
sides to each coin, you can help people,

00:43:14.322 --> 00:43:16.455
but those products can be damaging.

00:43:16.455 --> 00:43:20.238
I'm thinking about consumer debt,
you can give someone access to resources,

00:43:20.238 --> 00:43:21.970
but interest can be crippling.

00:43:21.970 --> 00:43:24.922
I'm curious how you think about,
what products to launch and

00:43:24.922 --> 00:43:27.092
what guardrails you
should put around them?

00:43:27.092 --> 00:43:31.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah, now, it's a really good question.

00:43:31.130 --> 00:43:36.091
I think going back to culture, knowing
that we are consumer obsessed first and

00:43:36.091 --> 00:43:40.525
we're doing the right thing for
the consumer allows us to move back and

00:43:40.525 --> 00:43:44.754
then define the product,
versus what happen in a lot of the banks.

00:43:44.754 --> 00:43:47.664
Which is, how do I make more
money by this product and

00:43:47.664 --> 00:43:51.368
just launch an hour fee or
increase the interest rate or you end up,

00:43:51.368 --> 00:43:54.961
what you see in most banks where
you have 110 different fees.

00:43:54.961 --> 00:43:57.308
If you look at one of
the big banks in Brazil,

00:43:57.308 --> 00:44:01.751
their fee table is 110 different things,
$0.02 here, $0.01 here,

00:44:01.751 --> 00:44:05.507
$0.10 here, just send me a text message,
they'll charge you.

00:44:05.507 --> 00:44:10.560
We went and said zeros,
our fee table is just a bunch of zeros.

00:44:10.560 --> 00:44:13.608
So we start with the simplicity and
transparency of the business model and

00:44:13.608 --> 00:44:16.657
figure out how we can to create
a sustainable business model that provides

00:44:16.657 --> 00:44:18.300
a lot of simplicity and transparency.

00:44:19.320 --> 00:44:26.660
And then specifically with credit,
there is a lot about aligning incentives.

00:44:26.660 --> 00:44:28.950
We've been fully aligned incentives.

00:44:28.950 --> 00:44:33.642
We only win if we provide a line
of credit that's going to work for

00:44:33.642 --> 00:44:39.620
a customer because we get to maintain
that customer for a very long time.

00:44:39.620 --> 00:44:43.196
If we provide the wrong product at
the wrong price for that customer,

00:44:43.196 --> 00:44:45.240
that customer will be in massive debt.

00:44:46.260 --> 00:44:51.317
But we also lose a very great customer
that we could have in our base.

00:44:51.317 --> 00:44:54.367
So that just means that
it's credit underwriting,

00:44:54.367 --> 00:44:59.165
it's fraud underwriting, it's using
other sophistication around modeling.

00:44:59.165 --> 00:45:04.772
It's a lot of simplicity in the language
about transparency in the communication

00:45:04.772 --> 00:45:09.821
with the customer, and always trying
to be fully aligned with him or her.

00:45:09.821 --> 00:45:13.727
&gt;&gt; Great, next question, over there.

00:45:17.952 --> 00:45:20.910
&gt;&gt; With class 2002.

00:45:20.910 --> 00:45:23.871
I'm a founder of venture capital
firm otter tech ventures.

00:45:23.871 --> 00:45:29.168
And so question to your cultural
behavioral managerial style.

00:45:29.168 --> 00:45:32.339
So a lot of companies
are struggling to transition from,

00:45:32.339 --> 00:45:36.037
let's call it flat organization
with free flow of information and

00:45:36.037 --> 00:45:40.085
very egalitarian decision making,
Into more structured businesses.

00:45:40.085 --> 00:45:43.551
Once you scale, you need to create
business units, you need to create

00:45:43.551 --> 00:45:47.800
responsibility, and accountability,
and the reporting verticals.

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:49.410
What would be your advice?

00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:53.987
At what point you say, okay, I cannot
be flat anymore, I have to verticalize?

00:45:53.987 --> 00:45:57.728
And do you have any recipes
how to protect the culture and

00:45:57.728 --> 00:46:02.990
still maintain the culture of this open
exchange as your organization scale?

00:46:02.990 --> 00:46:06.949
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a great question,
I said about the culture deck and

00:46:06.949 --> 00:46:11.292
how little has changed through the years,
a few things have changed.

00:46:11.292 --> 00:46:14.184
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And one of them was, again,

00:46:14.184 --> 00:46:17.165
going back to this mindset of
trying to reinvent everything and

00:46:17.165 --> 00:46:19.316
think everything from
a point of principle.

00:46:19.316 --> 00:46:23.820
We came in and said, we're a flat
organization, Deck said flat organization.

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:30.507
And that was a really bad idea.

00:46:30.507 --> 00:46:33.286
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It turns out that if you're completely

00:46:33.286 --> 00:46:36.231
flat and everybody has an idea,
it's complete chaos.

00:46:36.231 --> 00:46:38.264
And you become really slow, and

00:46:38.264 --> 00:46:43.590
you end up with these systems of consensus
where everybody needs to be agree.

00:46:43.590 --> 00:46:47.718
Turns out consensus is actually a driver
towards lower risk type of decisions,

00:46:47.718 --> 00:46:51.120
which means you'd end up with
a lower risk type of organization.

00:46:51.120 --> 00:46:56.564
And you slowed down your product.

00:46:56.564 --> 00:47:00.585
So, after seeing all of this happening and
you always go back and

00:47:00.585 --> 00:47:04.319
kind of question yourself about,
does this make any sense?

00:47:04.319 --> 00:47:07.834
And kind of the realization was
a bit is hierarchy is a good thing.

00:47:07.834 --> 00:47:10.570
There's fundamentally
nothing wrong with hierarchy.

00:47:10.570 --> 00:47:15.861
Hierarchy has existed in human
organizations for over 5,000 years.

00:47:15.861 --> 00:47:18.722
Almost every organization has a hierarchy,

00:47:18.722 --> 00:47:24.370
because hierarchy is very useful way
to organize a larger group of people.

00:47:24.370 --> 00:47:28.598
What's not good is hierarchies
that are created sometimes for

00:47:28.598 --> 00:47:32.267
other purposes,
rather than doing the right thing for

00:47:32.267 --> 00:47:36.270
the organization or
doing the right thing for the product.

00:47:36.270 --> 00:47:38.694
And what are some of those things?

00:47:38.694 --> 00:47:42.942
We remember going in Sao Paulo in our
building when we were renting a new

00:47:42.942 --> 00:47:46.974
building, and I had gotten a question
from the architect of our new

00:47:46.974 --> 00:47:51.080
building saying if we needed
a special elevator for the directors.

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:56.226
And if we needed to reserve the last floor
of the building for the directors, and

00:47:56.226 --> 00:48:00.020
I had no idea what that meant, until I
was invited for lunch at a big bank.

00:48:00.020 --> 00:48:05.477
And I saw myself going through a special
elevator to the top floor that was for

00:48:05.477 --> 00:48:06.671
directors, and

00:48:06.671 --> 00:48:11.626
I had waiters with white gloves
serving me lunch in a silver platter.

00:48:11.626 --> 00:48:16.768
Where everybody in the organization
else where it's not eating food, and

00:48:16.768 --> 00:48:21.840
so that is the wrong reason to build
hierarchies symbols of power and ego.

00:48:21.840 --> 00:48:27.333
And a lot of hierarchies end up being
co-opted by the ego of the founder,

00:48:27.333 --> 00:48:34.290
by the ego of the directors, of symbolling
powers, the big corner office of the CEO.

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:38.807
When you have a little cubicle, or
having a special parking lot for

00:48:38.807 --> 00:48:43.563
the CEO, I would love to go, and
I've never done it, I will someday,

00:48:43.563 --> 00:48:46.645
go to a CEO,
one of these big organizations.

00:48:46.645 --> 00:48:51.311
Explain to me why having that restaurant
only for you and that officer,

00:48:51.311 --> 00:48:55.450
how can you tie that directly
to better business performance?

00:48:57.040 --> 00:49:00.505
There isn't, there is no way to
justify those divisions through

00:49:00.505 --> 00:49:02.647
better business performance, is ego,

00:49:02.647 --> 00:49:07.001
so think that's when your organization
hierarchy ends up being a bit corrupted.

00:49:07.001 --> 00:49:09.615
Because it's just more ego and power, and

00:49:09.615 --> 00:49:14.190
titles that are driving a lot of layers
rather than doing the right thing for

00:49:14.190 --> 00:49:18.782
the organization, so, we have a hierarchy,
it's as flat as it becomes.

00:49:18.782 --> 00:49:22.213
We try to organize ourselves
in autonomous units,

00:49:22.213 --> 00:49:27.080
but with a clear leader that has real
accountability, we don't believe

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:31.660
anymore on group decision-making,
we want everybody's input.

00:49:31.660 --> 00:49:34.909
We want to hear everybody, but ultimately,
we've got to make a decision and

00:49:34.909 --> 00:49:38.340
there's got to be an accountable person
to make that decision, so we can move.

00:49:39.510 --> 00:49:43.899
We don't believe in consensus necessarily,
if consensus arrives, excellent,

00:49:43.899 --> 00:49:46.102
it doesn't, we have to make a decision.

00:49:46.102 --> 00:49:50.828
And then you have a hurricane with as many
layers with clear lines of accountability

00:49:50.828 --> 00:49:52.660
in the organization.

00:49:52.660 --> 00:49:56.190
And that allows you to drive fast,
but ultimately it's all

00:49:56.190 --> 00:50:00.708
about business performance and
driving to results, and it's as egoless,

00:50:00.708 --> 00:50:04.820
entitleless as possible, so
you can remove that from the day to day.

